iolent like it does and  so I thought that that kind of mother  relationship that she has at times  on-screen that the character of doctora  has Judea was able to portray and then  surprise us when she's able to you know  pay a heavy price to recover her liberty  and that's that that really blurs the  lines between victim and victimizer and  so that was something that Julianne  really really gave it all she had I mean  she was down there in Colombia for weeks  in a tent on a donkey in a raft and we I  mean we ran her through the model Israel  so you Lian you have been a stage  actress television actress film artist  don't different types of roles in your  life what attracted you to this  challenging role i thought the script  was amazing I thought it was unlike  anything I had ever read and I thought  that what Alejandra was just talking  about about young people and adolescence  and all that's raging through your body  paired with being in a war that felt  like a very exciting thing to explore  and I'd never played a role like this  and I loved the idea of going somewhere  else and and being in a new world and  and working with new people and I was  I'm so grateful to have been a part of  it it was not just an incredible work  experience but an incredible life  experience to have had this opportunity  once you land in Colombia please tell us  well we first went to the mountains  which was which felt sort of brutal in  its own way we stayed in a pretty simple  rustic retreat and I had I had like the  Queen suite up at the owner had a guest  bedroom but we would drive every day up  into chingus the national park and it  was an hour each way and of dirt road  with potholes the size of cars it was  very uncomfortable and when we'd get  there it was so beautiful but it was so  cold and so wet and every day people are  falling and  twelve thought how many it's about  thirteen it was really intense but then  that was like a walk in the park  compared to going to the jungle so I  tell people I'm from LA it's two planes  to Medellin and then you drive for three  hours on a paved road one hour on a dirt  road then you hike down an hour I opted  to not take the donkey down because I  heard it's scary going downhill on a  donkey and then a raft for an hour and  then we arrived at our camp and that's  where we were for three weeks with no  refrigeration no electricity no Wi-Fi  you know like you're we were pretty much  alone so what do you do when you have to  start interacting with this really very  diverse group of kids because these kids  if you look at their biographies some of  them are college students our kids come  from really dangerous neighborhoods a  real dichotomy of Colombian society you  know different social economic  backgrounds well basically we looked at  over 800 kids all around Colombia from  all sorts of places and we did it in  different ways we went to schools we  asked for videos over the Internet I  look for people when I was location  scouting I mean all that all the while  we were looking for this group and out  of the 800 I chose 30 let's say  finalists and we took them to this this  mountain camp that Julianne was  describing and we put them through  improv and acting exercises in the  morning in the afternoon they did  military exercises now we wanted them to  feel like a clandestine army so they  weren't doing any regular boot stomping  but different things sometimes barefoot  carrying a weapon and I found a  consultant a former guerrilla fighter  who had deserted the guerilla army and  he was there leading this so the kids  were actually sleeping together eating  together everything together and while  they were going through this sort of  mock boot camp I started to see the  chemistry's between them you know who  flirted with who who fought with who  and by seeing that many society form I  decide on and who would be the eight  Manos because the idea wasn't just how  each of them performed individually but 